From dc3fba212bd323358c4de951acdaae3b182a4ef8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel JB Clark <djbclark@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 15:44:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Added JsonParseStrict() and JsonParseFileStrict()

JsonObjectAppendElement() removes an existing member of the same name
before appending, so a document with two members of one name loses the
earlier one while it is being parsed, before the caller can look at it.
JsonParse() and everything built on it (JsonParseFile(), JsonParseAnyFile(),
JsonReadDataFile()) also stop at the end of the first value and ignore
whatever follows.

A caller that must not act on a document it only half-read, such as a
loader that validates every entry, needs both to be refused. The two new
functions do that: a name that an object already holds is refused with the
new JSON_PARSE_ERROR_OBJECT_DUPLICATE_KEY before the earlier member is
replaced, at any depth, and anything but whitespace after the value is
refused with JSON_PARSE_ERROR_INVALID_END. The file variant keeps the
size cap and also refuses a null byte, which would hide the rest of the
file from the parser.

This is opt-in: the existing functions behave exactly as before, with
duplicates resolved last-wins and a valid prefix accepted. The same
choice is made by Jansson as JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES, and RFC 8259
section 4 leaves duplicate names to the implementation.

